Count an expired dialog as dropped only if it was confirmed.

dlg_ontimeout() took every dialog it deleted off the active count and added
it to the expired count, an early dialog too. An early dialog is on the
early count, not the active one, so its expiry left the early gauge one
higher for good and pushed the active gauge one below the dialogs that are
up. The expired counter is what the IMS session drop rate (TS 32.454 5.2.1,
dropped over established sessions) reads, and a call that rang and then
timed out before an answer is not a dropped session.

Do what the BYE path does: an early dialog leaves the early count, and only
a confirmed one goes off the active count and onto the expired one.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_dialog/dlg_handlers.c b/src/modules/ims_dialog/dlg_handlers.c
index 6ba63b2c14..5c18c991ff 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_dialog/dlg_handlers.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_dialog/dlg_handlers.c
@@ -1939,8 +1939,15 @@ void dlg_ontimeout(struct dlg_tl *tl)
 		run_dlg_callbacks(DLGCB_EXPIRED, dlg, NULL, NULL, DLG_DIR_NONE, 0);
 
 		unref_dlg(dlg, unref + 1);
-		counter_add(dialog_ng_cnts_h.active, -1);
-		counter_inc(dialog_ng_cnts_h.expired);
+		/* the same balance as a BYE: an early dialog is on the early
+		 * count, not the active one, and it never was a session that
+		 * could be dropped */
+		if(old_state == DLG_STATE_EARLY) {
+			counter_add(dialog_ng_cnts_h.early, -1);
+		} else {
+			counter_add(dialog_ng_cnts_h.active, -1);
+			counter_inc(dialog_ng_cnts_h.expired);
+		}
 	} else {
 		unref_dlg(dlg, 1);
 	}
